> I have started a plan to build an abstraction around model and tool selection in my AI system. Here is the contract for tools:
> """
> Tool protocol and normalized typed tool-result contract.
> 
> Tools are executable capabilities. In the initial synchronous system they may
> be invoked directly by a responder. In the workflow system they are executed
> as Operations and yield Artifacts. Tool interfaces should therefore avoid
> assuming that their primary result is user-visible text.
> 
> Expected tool callable shape:
> 
> ToolCallable = Callable[
>     [ToolInvocation],
>     ToolResult,
> ]
> 
> @dataclass(frozen=True)
> class ToolInvocation:
>     operation_id: str | None
>     conversation_id: str
>     arguments: Mapping[str, Any]
>     config: Mapping[str, Any]
>     context: PlannerContext | None
>     provenance_requirements: Mapping[str, Any]
> 
> @dataclass
> class Tool:
>     name: str
>     description: str
>     fn: ToolCallable | Callable[..., Any]
>     config: ToolConfig
>     quality_prior: float | None = None
>     cost_estimate: float | None = None
>     version: str = "1"
> 
> Compatibility:
> - registry.py may accept legacy functions such as
>   `fn(prompt, config) -> str`.
> - tools/base.py normalizes such outputs to ToolResult/Artifact.
> - Tool configuration defaults and allowed discrete options are validated here.
> 
> Caller:
> - responders.py or workflow/workers.py invokes:
>       result = tool.fn(invocation)
> - the caller persists raw temporary output as appropriate, converts relevant
>   materials to artifacts/source excerpts, and never blindly injects untrusted
>   tool text into system instructions.
> """
> 
> Now, here is the documentation for a tool available through OpenAI:
> ## Programmatic tool calling
> 
> `programmatic_tool_calling` lets the model write JavaScript that calls your other tools in loops or in parallel, then returns one compact result. It runs in an isolated V8 runtime with no network, filesystem or Node.js; it reaches the outside only through the tools you enable.
> 
> ```python
> tools = [
>     {"type": "function", "name": "get_inventory",
>      "description": "Return sku and available_units.",
>      "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"sku": {"type": "string"}},
>                     "required": ["sku"], "additionalProperties": False},
>      "output_schema": {"type": "object",
>                        "properties": {"sku": {"type": "string"},
>                                       "available_units": {"type": "number"}},
>                        "required": ["sku", "available_units"],
>                        "additionalProperties": False},
>      "allowed_callers": ["programmatic"]},
>     {"type": "programmatic_tool_calling"},
> ]
> resp = client.responses.create(model="gpt-6-astra", tools=tools,
>                                input="Compare inventory with demand for sku_123.")
> ```
> 
> The hosted tool itself takes no options in the Responses API; control sits on each callable tool:
> 
> | Field on other tools | Values | Effect |
> | --- | --- | --- |
> | `allowed_callers` | omitted or `["direct"]` | Model calls it directly only |
> |  | `["programmatic"]` | Only from a program |
> |  | `["direct", "programmatic"]` | Either way |
> | `output_schema` | JSON Schema | Lets the program rely on return fields |
> 
> Eligible tool types: `function`, `custom`, `mcp`, `apply_patch`, `shell` and `code_interpreter`. Responses contain `program`, nested `function_call` (with a `caller` field you must echo in your `function_call_output`) and `program_output` items. In the Agents API it is on by default; disable with `{"type": "programmatic_tool_calling", "enabled": false}`.
> 
> Can you construct a full tool implementation for this? Please start off the file with a docstring, describing the tool and its usage.
